senators get one last chance to question  supreme court nominee amy coney barrett  in a marathon confirmation hearing  yesterday senate judiciary committee  members  grilled the appeals court judge for  nearly 12  hours like other recent nominees barrett  did not discuss how she would decide  cases  on the high court she repeatedly made  the point that she has  no policy agenda and did not want to be  used as  a quote pawn nancy cordes is covering  the barrett hearings nancy what can we  expect today  well anthony today senators on the  committee will get 20 minutes  each to follow up on questions that they  feel were not answered yesterday which  as far as democrats are concerned covers  a lot of territory  but republicans like the fact that  barrett is being cautious  they think that barring some major  stumble today  victory is in sight i have made no  precommitments to anyone about how i  would decide a case  barrett insisted tuesday she does not  have it out for obamacare i'm  not here on a mission to destroy the  affordable care act  but democratic vp nominee kamala harris  argued barrett's past criticisms  including a 2017 article suggests  otherwise  how many months after you published that  article  did president trump nominate you to be a  judge on the court of appeals  i believe my nomination to the court of  appeals was announced  in may of 2017.  that's correct but i don't remember when  the article came out  the article was published in january of  2017 so that would have been five months  later  a hearing for a supreme court justice  has never been held  this close to a presidential election  will you commit to recusing yourself  from any case arising from a dispute in  the presidential election  results three weeks from now i certainly  hope that all members of the committee  have more confidence in my integrity  than to think that i would allow myself  to be used as a pawn to decide this  election  democrats tried unsuccessfully tuesday  to pin barrett down  on issues like abortion are you willing  to say that roe was correctly decided  it's inconsistent with the duties of a  sitting judge  to take positions on cases that the  court has decided in the past  louisiana republican john kennedy argued  that judges personal views are  irrelevant  because they take an oath of  impartiality anyway  are you going to take that oath and  affirm it  if you're confirmed yes  you're not lying i'm not lying i took  that oath before  i began as a judge on the seventh  circuit and i have not violated that  oath and i would  take it again and oaths are serious  to me there were some tense moments  tuesday after  barrett declined to say whether she  agreed with the court's decision  legalizing same-sex marriage i do want  to be clear  that i have never discriminated on the  basis of sexual preference  hawaii senator maisie hirono sexual  preference is an offensive and outdated  term  it is used by anti-lgbtq activists  to suggest that sexual orientation is a  choice  i certainly didn't mean and you know  would never mean  to use a term that would cause any  offense in the lgbtq community  so if i did i greatly apologize for that  at one point during the hearing judge  barrett was asked to hold up the notes  that she had in front of her and  you can see she held up a blank page  which was meant to highlight the fact  that she was answering  hours and hours of questions about  detailed legal arguments  without any doubts at all tony blank  page but not a blank mind nancy thank  you very much
 

